Apple PPC64 Mach-O relocation oracles
====================================

probe.macho is an unmodified copy of Apple's probe.o (batch 9).
carry.macho is an unmodified copy of Apple's carry.o (batch 10).
The .macho extension avoids ignoring object files; it does not change bytes.
The companion .s files are the original Apple assembler sources, not Go asm.

Captured on Power Mac G5 / Mac OS X 10.5.8, Xcode 3.1.4, Apple assembler;
original bundles: g5-abi-batch9.tar.gz and g5-abi-batch10.tar.gz in the port
project's out/g5-leopard-abi-20261004/. Reproduction with the era's gcc
assembler driver: gcc -arch ppc64 -c probe.s -o probe.o (carry.s analogously).
These are measured golden objects, never generated by the Go writer under test.

SHA-256 of whole objects:
35e9c41a0b8c6c3675f02d327b0a5f64b7fc8001fd635f687073c1854d024d91  probe.macho
f442d46a46d11ca6eb5b5e9729d46c81148aff9f9ed123d8a0c52308a24365be  carry.macho

Both text sections begin at file offset 288. Relocations:
probe.macho[312:384] = all 9 records / 72 bytes.
carry.macho[324:452] = all 16 records / 128 bytes.
Apple nlist indices: probe _ext=1, _global=2; carry _g=1.

TestMachoreloc1Apple calls the actual instruction-addend preparation and
relocation writer for these source shapes. Only order is normalised: atomic
primary+PAIR references (BR24 is unpaired) are sorted by descending primary
section offset. No record field or word is masked or skipped. A negative
control flips one symbolnum bit in one PAIR bitfield word and must be rejected
by the same complete-byte equality comparison, in both operand directions.

This does not test an external-link/cgo workflow or scattered relocations.
